      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to determine the condition under which \\(-2\\) is a possible output of the function. This involves solving the equation:\n\n\\[ f(x) = -2 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( f(x) \\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\]\n\nRearranging terms to isolate \\( c \\), we obtain:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\]\n\nFor \\(-2\\) to be in the range of \\( f(x) \\), this quadratic equation must have at least one real solution. Therefore, the discriminant of the quadratic equation must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac \\]\n\nIn our equation \\( x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\), the coefficients are \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8 \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 1 - 4c \\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have real solutions, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[ 1 - 4c \\geq 0 \\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[ 1 \\geq 4c \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\geq c \\]\n\\[ c \\leq \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nThe largest value of \\( c \\) that satisfies this inequality is:\n\n\\[ c = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
