      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\( -2 \\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to solve for \\( c \\) in the equation \\( f(x) = -2 \\). This means we need to find \\( c \\) such that:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in the form \\( x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\). For \\( x \\) to be real, the discriminant of this quadratic equation must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nIn our equation, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) = 9 - 4(c + 2) = 9 - 4c - 8 = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor \\( x \\) to be real, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\geq c\n\\]\n\\[\nc \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\( -2 \\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
